  "query_preview": "/THURSDAY THROUGH SUNDAY/...\nIssued at 231 AM MDT Mon Sep 21 2026\n\nThursday onwards the CWA looked to remain largely in between the \nupper-level ridge to the south and east and an incoming upper-level \ntrough to the west and north. Southwesterly flow aloft is expected \nto continue, with the Pacific Ocean fetch once again setting up \nacross the western CONUS. A few lobes and bands of 500mb vorticity \nwill move through the region throughout the end of the week, but \nprecipitation chances remain quite low through the weekend. 700mb \nflow will remain variable and fairly weak Thursday through the \nweekend, leading to a much quieter end of the week as compared to \nthe start of the week. 700mb temperatures remain between about 8 and \n10C Thursday through the weekend, favoring seasonable, if not \nslightly above average, temperatures. Daily high temperatures will \nbe in the 60s and 70s for most of the area, with 80s possible in the \nNorth Platte River Valley and across much of the Nebraska Panhandle. \nSaturday looks to be the warmest day, as westerly 700mb flow \npromotes some weak downsloping along and east of the Laramie Range \nand downslope warming increasing temperatures to the east. With the \nwarmer temperatures, afternoon minimum humidity values will start to \ndrop back into the 25 to 30 percent range to end the weak, leading \nto slightly elevated fire concerns Thursday onwards. Red Flag \nWarnings are not anticipated at this time, as the southwesterly \nfetch of moisture should keep the surface moist enough to prevent \ncritical humidity values from developing. Additionally, overnight \nhumidity recoveries will be above about 70 to 80% most night through \nthe weekend.",
